  "@type": "Capability",
  "critical": false,
  "description": "Nuclear Thermal Propulsion (NTP): a fission reactor heats a low-molecular-weight propellant (typically liquid hydrogen) directly to 2500-3000 K, exhausting it through a nozzle to achieve 850-1000 s specific impulse — two to three times chemical performance. Unlike Nuclear Electric Propulsion, NTP produces high thrust (tens to hundreds of kN) at moderate Isp, enabling fast manned transits. Heritage: NERVA (NRX, Pewee, Phoebus-2A at 4 GW thermal) demonstrated 850 s Isp in the 1960s.",
  "early_win": false,
